[{"category":"section","location":"numerics/#Numerical-behavior","page":"Numerical contracts","text":"CD's private PB SparseIterate retains stored activation/compaction order. First full passes admit coordinates, active passes refine them, and only a full pass strictly below the change tolerance converges. Random LS uses historical momentum/restarts and refreshes moved residual caches before full checks; the borrowed RNG advances. Cyclic and explicit momentum=false remain supported. Zero columns choose the nearest-zero penalty minimizer, including lambda=0. Nonzero-column curvature underflow is an error. tol=0 stays strict; maxpasses=0 returns unconverged with no updates. A nonfinite returned objective is refused before dense scatter; this is not robust dynamic-range recovery.\n\nPGD retains native LS image/value/gradient order, scalar steps, fused PB prox, TFOCS objective-to-gradient checks, finite curvature-growth refusal and zero-step gradient refresh. Historical objective/iterate/FASTA convergence tests combine by OR. Converged does not guarantee KKT below gradtol; objective rounding can stop even with very small ftol. Check objective/KKT independently. Native Float32 may stagnate earlier. Finite inputs do not ensure finite intermediates; historical LS image exceptional-range limitations remain outside this extraction.\n\nCallbacks/display sample printevery; stored traces include each iteration. A callback receives the trace when storing, otherwise a state. Limits are checked after an iteration and counts may overshoot. iterationconverged means a limit, not success. Positive gaptol is refused; positive gapevery is inert at gaptol=0. The retained scalar APIs/results do not introduce a new stopping/logging engine.","title":"Numerical behavior"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-reference","page":"API","text":"","title":"API reference"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ProximalSolvers.ProximalSolver","page":"API","text":"ProximalSolver\n\nAbstract supertype of CDSolver and ProximalGradientDescent, passed to solve!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ProximalSolvers.ProximalSolver"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ProximalSolvers.CDSolver","page":"API","text":"CDSolver(; maxpasses=100_000, tol=1e-7, order=:random, relative=true,\n           momentum=nothing, rng=Random.default_rng(), anderson=0,\n           gaptol=0, gapsafe=false)\n\nDense least-squares lasso coordinate descent. A full pass admits coordinates; active passes refine them until another full pass checks strict change stopping. Random LS uses historical momentum by default. Set momentum=false to disable it; cyclic order refuses explicit momentum. The borrowed RNG advances between solves. Anderson, gap certificates and screening are deferred and explicitly rejected.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ProximalSolvers.CDSolver"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ProximalSolvers.ProximalGradientDescent","page":"API","text":"ProximalGradientDescent(; ls=LineSearches.BackTracking(), normalized=false,\n                         debias=0, preconditioner=:none)\n\nHistorical scalar-step dense LS lasso PGD, retaining its LS image cache, fused PB proximal call, TFOCS backtracking and FASTA/x/objective OR stopping. Normalized IHT, debiasing, preconditioning and custom searches are deferred.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ProximalSolvers.ProximalGradientDescent"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ProximalSolvers.solve!","page":"API","text":"solve!(x, f, g, CDSolver())\nsolve!(x, f, g, ProximalGradientDescent(), options=ProximalOptions())\n\nOverwrite dense Vector{T} x, T=Float32 or Float64, for same-type Matrix X/Vector y and unweighted finite nonnegative NormL1. Use (one(T)/T(n))*LeastSquaresLoss for normalized lasso; raw LS keeps its unnormalized meaning. One positive finite scalar LS wrapper is supported. Borrowed X/y must not alias mutable x or change during a solve. Results share x; copy it to retain earlier coefficients. Unsupported inputs/options fail before mutation. Convergence uses historical stopping tests, not a KKT certificate. Finite intermediate arithmetic is required. CD errors before scatter preserve input x; PGD step errors restore its last accepted x and rethrow, without a transaction guarantee for internal counters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ProximalSolvers.solve!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ProximalSolvers.ProximalOptions","page":"API","text":"ProximalOptions(; maxiter=200, ftol=1e-6, xtol=1e-8, gradtol=1e-5,\n  allow_f_increases=false, store_trace=false, show_trace=false,\n  extended_trace=false, print_every=1, callback=nothing, time_limit=Inf,\n  f_calls_limit=0, grad_calls_limit=0, prox_calls_limit=0, gaptol=0, gapevery=10)\n\nPGD retains objective-change, iterate-change OR FASTA residual stopping. This convergence flag is not a KKT certificate. Callback/display cadence is print_every; stored traces contain each iteration. A callback sees the trace when storing, otherwise the current state. Extended metadata copies x and its gradient. Call/time limits are checked after complete iterations, so counts may exceed limits by one iteration; zero call limits mean unlimited. Native Float32/64 buffers retain their arithmetic; scalar diagnostics use Float64. Tolerances below working precision do not imply corresponding coefficient accuracy. Positive gaptol is deferred and refused. Positive gapevery stays inert at gaptol=0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ProximalSolvers.ProximalOptions"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ProximalSolvers.CDResults","page":"API","text":"CDResults\n\nResult sharing the caller's vector as minimizer, with minimum, passes, converged, maxchange and tolerance. Compatibility fields kkt_checks=0 and gap=NaN are sentinels, never computed certificates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ProximalSolvers.CDResults"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ProximalSolvers.OptimizationState","page":"API","text":"OptimizationState{T<:ProximalSolver}\n\nOne iteration of a trace: iteration, the objective fg_x = f(x) + g(x), the relative change dxx = ‖x - x'‖ / ‖x‖, the inverse step size L, and a metadata dictionary that holds the iterate and gradient when extended_trace = true.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ProximalSolvers.OptimizationState"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ProximalSolvers.OptimizationTrace","page":"API","text":"OptimizationTrace{T}\n\nA Vector{OptimizationState{T}}, stored in the results when store_trace = true (see ProximalOptions).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ProximalSolvers.OptimizationTrace"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ProximalSolvers.OptimizationResults","page":"API","text":"OptimizationResults\n\nQualified PGD result. minimizer aliases x; minimum, iterations, convergence flags, residuals/tolerances, trace, call and backtracking counts retain their historical meanings. iterationconverged means an iteration limit, not success. backtracksteps includes accepted nonzero trials. gap is a NaN sentinel, never a computed certificate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ProximalSolvers.OptimizationResults"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ProximalSolvers.LineSearches.BackTracking","page":"API","text":"BackTracking(; α = 0.9, β = 0.5, Lexact = Inf)\n\nThe backtracking line search of TFOCS (private/tfocs_backtrack.m). Each iteration starts from L ← α L, where 1/L is the step size, and while the local Lipschitz estimate L̂ of the step exceeds L it retries with L ← min(Lexact, max(L̂, L / β)). L̂ comes from function values until they are too close to compare in floating point, and from gradients after that. An LS trial whose image or value overflows is never accepted: it gives no estimate, and the search retries with L ← L / β.\n\nThe object holds settings only and can be shared between solves; the bookkeeping lives in the solver state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ProximalSolvers.LineSearches.BackTracking"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/02_pgd_lasso/#Proximal-gradient-lasso","page":"Proximal gradient lasso","text":"The same lasso with the historical cached proximal-gradient algorithm.\n\nusing LinearAlgebra, ProximalBase, ProximalSolvers\nX = [1. 0.; 0. 2.; 0. 0.]\ny = [2., -2., .5]\nx = zeros(2)\nf = (1/length(y)) * LeastSquaresLoss(y, X)\ng = NormL1(.1)\noptions = ProximalOptions(maxiter=2000, ftol=1e-14, xtol=1e-12, gradtol=1e-10)\nresult = solve!(x, f, g, ProximalGradientDescent(), options)\n@assert result.converged && result.minimizer === x\n\nAccuracy is checked separately from the solver's stopping flag.\n\n@assert isapprox(x, [1.7, -.925]; atol=1e-7)\nprintln(result)\nResults of Optimization Algorithm\n * Algorithm: Proximal Gradient Descent\n * Minimizer: [1.699999938361624,-0.9250000073472205]\n * Minimum: 3.229167e-01\n * Iterations: 26\n * Convergence: true\n   * |x - x'| < 1.0e-12: false\n     |x - x'| = 4.23e-08\n   * |f(x) - f(x')| / |f(x)| < 1.0e-14: true\n     |f(x) - f(x')| / |f(x)| = 3.27e-15\n   * |g(x)| < 1.0e-10: false\n     |g(x)| = 2.05e-08\n   * stopped by an increasing objective: false\n   * Reached Maximum Number of Iterations: false\n   * Reached a limit on the number of calls: false\n * Backtracking steps: 29\n * f(x) calls: 56\n * ∇f(x) calls: 27\n * prox calls: 29","title":"Proximal gradient lasso"},{"category":"section","location":"support/#Supported-API,-ownership-and-migration","page":"Support and migration","text":"Only Vector x/y and Matrix X with the same Float32 or Float64 element type are supported. Require positive consistent dimensions and finite entries. Use raw PB LS or one positive finite scalar Scaled LS, and unweighted finite nonnegative NormL1. Parameters must be representable; positive values must not round to zero. Scaling is explicit: raw LS means sum(abs2,y-X*x)/2; divide by n explicitly for normalized lasso. Accepted wider scalars retain historical conversion sites.\n\nX/y are borrowed read-only during solves and must not alias mutable x. Results share x; extended traces copy x/gradient. Unsupported requests fail before mutation. CD exceptions before scatter preserve input x; PGD numerical step exceptions restore its last accepted point and rethrow. Counters/buffers are not generally transactional. Shape errors are DimensionMismatch; unsupported combinations/options are informative ArgumentError.\n\nSparse inputs/designs, views, weighted/group penalties, matrix responses, intercepts/offsets, generalized losses/compositions, acceleration, ADMM/Newton, primal-dual/SLOPE/graphical models, paths/screening/certificates/workspaces/refit and reweighting are deferred. Nondefault feature requests are refused. gap=NaN and CD kkt_checks=0 are compatibility sentinels, not computed diagnostics.\n\nThis is a breaking support reduction. Version/registration and any public release remain a separate decision; existing releases are untouched. Protected archive refs preserve the complete snapshot; legacy/full remains the historical working branch. Restore capabilities selectively; do not merge it wholesale.","title":"Supported API, ownership and migration"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Architecture-and-validation","page":"Architecture","text":"The twelve active files are module/types/blocks/linesearches/solve/utils/precompile, solvers/imagemodels and proximalgradientdescent, and cd/models/solver/leastsquares. Small native helpers support two separately cached algorithms and existing result types. CDModel and state hooks are private extraction/test seams, not promises of custom loss or workspace support. Generic block/metric/certificate/ logging frameworks are deferred.\n\nThe current exact PB pin is 2b373c546e247781d9514a7000ef3e010343ca4c. CD uses its scalar prox_block oracle; PGD retains fused value_and_prox!. The private PB SparseIterate contract remains unchanged. PR #237's group/workspace migration is not imported. The gated analysis job always runs isolated exact-source paired diagnostics against PB A e8a45a8a6ffe2a6f92d83c4c77d62a9b7631e291 and frozen PS 64930401cfc0451e886728d916263c4dab2baf36, including when package tests are not selected or reused. Loaded paths, clean Git HEADs and source digests are checked; version strings never skip probes. C0 and the provenance audit describe the independent full-suite mathematical/behavioral tests.\n\nCurrent CI is stable Julia1 Linux x64 only for every tier. Preserve required checks, full master validation, coverage, benchmark-analysis and conservative policy-aware nightly reuse. Historical platform runs and the deferred macOS C0 warm-convergence failure remain historical, not newly passing claims.\n\nHistorical measured performance tables remain in the repository's full-package performance record. They are not replaced by unmeasured minimal-core claims.","title":"Architecture and validation"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/01_cd_lasso/#Coordinate-descent-lasso","page":"Coordinate descent lasso","text":"Coordinate descent for an explicitly normalized dense lasso.\n\nusing LinearAlgebra, ProximalBase, ProximalSolvers\nX = [1. 0.; 0. 2.; 0. 0.]\ny = [2., -2., .5]\nx = zeros(2)\nf = (1/length(y)) * LeastSquaresLoss(y, X)\ng = NormL1(.1)\nresult = solve!(x, f, g, CDSolver())\n@assert result.converged && result.minimizer === x\n\nIndependent orthogonal-column solution, with unequal column norms.\n\n@assert isapprox(x, [1.7, -.925]; atol=1e-7)\nprintln(result)\nResults of Optimization Algorithm\n * Algorithm: Coordinate Descent\n * Minimum: 3.229167e-01\n * Passes: 3\n * Convergence: true\n   * largest change in the last pass 0.00e+00 (tolerance 1.66e-07)","title":"Coordinate descent lasso"},{"category":"section","location":"#ProximalSolvers.jl","page":"Home","text":"Ordinary dense least-squares lasso through CDSolver, ProximalGradientDescent, and solve!. ProximalBase provides LeastSquaresLoss and NormL1. The minimal core preserves historical numerical kernels while deliberately narrowing support. See support/migration, numerical contracts and both executable examples before using the package.","title":"ProximalSolvers.jl"},{"category":"module","location":"#ProximalSolvers","page":"Home","text":"ProximalSolvers\n\nDense least-squares lasso using CDSolver or ProximalGradientDescent through solve!. Losses, proximal operators and private coordinate storage belong to PB.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ProximalSolvers"}]
